# vmlab: what stays out of git
#
# How to read this file
#   - Git treats "#" as a comment only at the START of a line, so every note sits on its own line above its pattern.
#   - A leading "/" anchors a pattern to the repository root. Without it, the pattern matches at any depth.
#   - A trailing "/" matches folders only.
#   - To see which rule hides a path:   git check-ignore -v PATH
#   - To list tracked files a rule now hides:   git ls-files -ci --exclude-standard
#
# The sections run from "never commit this" down to "just clutter".


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Secrets and sign-ins. Never safe to commit, not even to a private repo.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Environment files. Tokens and passwords are read from the environment, never from a file in the repo.
.env
.env.*
# ...except a template that holds only names and made-up values, if one is ever added.
!.env.example

# Keys and certificates.
*.pem
*.key
*.pfx
*.p12

# Azure CLI sign-in state. vmlab keeps the real ones in a Docker volume (azure-token), outside the repo and
# outside the image. These patterns catch a copy that lands here by mistake, for example `just adopt-login` run
# by hand with the wrong folder.
.azure/
azureProfile.json
msal_token_cache.json
msal_http_cache.bin
service_principal_entries.json


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Your own settings and the records of what you ran. Local to your computer.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Your overrides of vmlab.yaml, such as your licence statement and its case number. vmlab.yaml holds the defaults.
/vmlab.local.yaml

# Run records: the exact values used, every step, every output, and any files brought back from a machine. They
# can hold a project's own output, so they are never committed.
/runs/

# Anything you save while trying something out by hand.
/scratch/
/tmp/


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Things the tools make. They are rebuilt from the sources.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Node packages. The code runs from source in Docker, so there is no build folder to commit.
node_modules/
.pnpm-store/

# TypeScript's incremental build record, and any build or test output.
*.tsbuildinfo
dist/
build/
coverage/
.nyc_output/

# Installers and archives fetched while trying something out. A suite names its downloads in its own script, with
# a pinned hash, so none of these belongs in the repo.
*.exe
*.msi
*.msix
*.zip


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Editors, assistants and the operating system. Personal to whoever is working here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Editors.
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Claude Code: local settings and working copies. (CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md ARE committed.)
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/worktrees/

# macOS.
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride

# Windows.
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini

# Logs.
*.log
npm-debug.log*
pnpm-debug.log*
